    model: 'An ECG monitor detects the heart’s electrical activity, which normally begins at the SA node and spreads through the AV node, bundle of His, bundle branches and Purkinje fibers to produce P, QRS and T waves. Ventricular tachycardia is a dangerously fast rhythm originating in the ventricles, so the monitor alarms when it detects rapid, wide complexes. Chewing and brushing contract skeletal muscles near the electrodes, and that muscle electrical activity (artifact) can look like rapid, wide complexes, so the monitor misreads it as V-tach, while the patient remains well. Electrodes placed over large muscles, on poorly prepared skin or with dried gel pick up more artifact. The clinical team should first assess the patient directly, then check that electrodes are fresh, properly placed on flat, bony areas with good skin prep, and that lead wires and cables are intact; the BMET can verify filter settings and monitor performance with a patient simulator and document the findings.',
