  frq: {
    prompt: 'A bedside monitor connected to a patient with a central venous catheter and temporary pacing wires fails its electrical safety inspection: ground resistance is 2.4 Ω and chassis leakage is high. Explain why this is especially dangerous for this patient, what each failed test indicates, what likely causes it, and what the BMET must do before the device returns to service.',
    rubric: [['Explains microshock risk for electrically susceptible patients with direct cardiac paths (~100 µA)', ['microshock', 'direct', 'heart', 'catheter', 'pacing', 'susceptible']], ['Interprets failed ground resistance (exceeds 0.5 Ω) and high leakage (current not safely grounded)', ['0.5', 'ground', 'leakage', 'path']], ['Identifies likely causes (damaged cord, loose ground connection, failed insulation or component)', ['cord', 'pin', 'loose', 'insulation', 'damaged']], ['Describes actions (remove from service, tag, repair/replace cord, retest normal and single-fault, document; LOTO/safety during repair)', ['remove', 'tag', 'repair', 'replace', 'retest', 'document']]],
    model: 'This patient is electrically susceptible: the central venous catheter and temporary pacing wires create a low-resistance path directly to the heart, where currents as small as about 100 microamps can cause ventricular fibrillation (microshock). The ground resistance of 2.4 ohms exceeds the NFPA 99 limit of 0.5 ohm, meaning the protective ground path is degraded, and the high chassis leakage means unintended current is present on the enclosure. Together, leakage current may not be safely returned through the ground wire and could instead flow through the patient or a staff member who touches the monitor and the patient’s leads. Likely causes include a damaged power cord, a loose or corroded ground connection or broken ground pin, or deteriorating insulation or a failing internal component. The BMET must immediately remove the monitor from service and tag it, isolate it from power while working, replace the cord or repair the ground and any faulty component per the service manual, then repeat ground resistance, chassis and patient leakage tests under normal and single-fault conditions to confirm they meet limits, verify monitor performance, and document all findings and repairs before returning it to service.',
  },
  sources: [['OpenStax College Physics 2e, 20.6 Electric Hazards and the Human Body', PHY + '20-6-electric-hazards-and-the-human-body'], ['OpenStax College Physics 2e, 23.8 Electrical Safety: Systems and Devices', PHY + '23-8-electrical-safety-systems-and-devices'], ['OSHA: Control of Hazardous Energy (Lockout/Tagout)', 'https://www.osha.gov/control-hazardous-energy'], ['OSHA: Hazard Communication', 'https://www.osha.gov/hazcom'], ['CDC: Guideline for Disinfection and Sterilization in Healthcare Facilities', 'https://www.cdc.gov/infection-control/hcp/disinfection-and-sterilization/index.html'], ['American College of Radiology: MR Safety', 'https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Radiology-Safety/MR-Safety']],
